 the parity check method:\n\n1. **Identify the Parity of Each Number**:\n   - Determine whether each number in the addition is odd or even.\n   - An odd number ends in 1, 3, 5, 7, or 9.\n   - An even number ends in 0, 2, 4, 6, or 8.\n\n2. **Sum the Parities**:\n   - Add the parities of all the numbers involved in the addition.\n   - For example, if you have the numbers 137931054 and 871843706, you would identify their parities:\n     - 137931054 is even (ends in 4).\n     - 871843706 is even (ends in 6).\n\n3. **Calculate the Sum of Parities**:\n   - Add the parities of the two numbers:\n     - Even + Even = Even\n\n4. **Check the Parity of the Sum**:\n   - The sum of the two numbers should have the same parity as the sum of their parities.\n   - In this case, the sum of the parities is Even (Even + Even = Even).\n   - The sum of the numbers is 1009774760, which is also even.\n\n5. **Conclusion**:\n   - Since the sum of the parities (Even + Even = Even) matches the parity of the sum (1009774760 is Even), the addition is correct.\n\nBy following this method, you can quickly verify the correctness of the sum without performing the full addition again. This method is particularly useful for mental calculations or quick checks. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide an example of a similar problem where the parity check method would be useful?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's consider a similar problem where the parity check method would be useful. Suppose we have the numbers 123456789 and 987654321. We will use the parity check method to verify the sum.\n\n1. **Identify the Parity of Each Number**:\n   - 123456789: The last digit is 9, so it is odd.\n   - 987654321: The last digit is 1, so it is odd.\n\n2. **Sum the Parities**:\n   - Add the parities of the two numbers:\n     - Odd + Odd = Even\n\n3. **Calculate the Sum of Parities**:\n   - The sum of the parities is Even (Odd + Odd = Even).\n\n4. **Check the Parity of the Sum**:\n   - Now, let's find the sum of the numbers:\n     \\[\n     123456789 + 987654321 = 1111111110\n     \\]\n   - The last digit of 1111111110 is 0, so it is even.\n\n5. **Conclusion**:\n   - The sum of the parities (Even + Odd = Even) matches the parity of the sum (1111111110 is Even), confirming that the addition is correct.\n\nBy using the parity check method, we can quickly verify that the sum of 123456789 and 987654321 is indeed 1111111110. This method is particularly useful for mental calculations or quick checks, especially when dealing with large numbers. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide another example where the parity check method is useful?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's consider another example where the parity check method is useful. Suppose we have the numbers 24680 and 75320. We will use the parity check method to verify the sum.\n\n1. **Identify the Parity of Each Number**:\n   - 24680: The last digit is 0, so it is even.\n   - 75320: The last digit is 0, so it is even.\n\n2. **Sum the Parities**:\n   - Add the parities of the two numbers:\n     - Even + Even = Even\n\n3. **Calculate the Sum of Parities**:\n   - The sum of the parities is Even (Even + Even = Even).\n\n4. **Check the Parity of the Sum**:\n   - Now, let's find the sum of the numbers:\n     \\[\n     24680 + 75320 = 100000\n     \\]\n   - The last digit of 100000 is 0, so it is even.\n\n5. **Conclusion**:\n   - The sum of the parities (Even + Even = Even) matches the parity of the sum (100000 is Even), confirming that the addition is correct.\n\nBy using the parity check method, we can quickly verify that the sum of 24680 and 75320 is indeed 100000. This method is particularly useful for mental calculations or quick checks, especially when dealing with large numbers or when you need to verify the correctness of a sum without performing the full addition again. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide another example where the parity check method is useful, but with a different approach to verify the sum?\n<\uff5cAssist