cdot 4 = 12\n     \\]\n   - Then, calculate \\( 2 \\cdot 12 \\):\n     \\[\n     2 \\cdot 12 = 24\n     \\]\n\nSo, the result of \\( (2 \\cdot 3) \\cdot 4 \\) is \\( 24 \\), and the result of \\( 2 \\cdot (3 \\cdot 4) \\) is also \\( 24 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{24}\n\\]\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation and example. It really helps to see the associative property of multiplication in action.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask. Understanding the associative property of multiplication is crucial for simplifying expressions and solving more complex problems. If you have any other topics or questions, let me know! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Could you provide an example of a problem where you need to use the commutative property, similar to the addition and subtraction problems we've been working on?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! The commutative property states that the order of the numbers does not affect the result of the operation. This property applies to both addition and multiplication. For addition, it is expressed as \\( a + b = b + a \\), and for multiplication, it is expressed as \\( a \\cdot b = b \\cdot a \\).\n\nLet's solve a problem using the commutative property of multiplication. We'll use the expression \\( 5 \\cdot 7 \\).\n\n1. **Apply the commutative property**:\n   - The commutative property of multiplication tells us that the order of the numbers does not matter:\n     \\[\n     5 \\cdot 7 = 7 \\cdot 5\n     \\]\n\n2. **Perform the multiplication**:\n   - Calculate \\( 5 \\cdot 7 \\):\n     \\[\n     5 \\cdot 7 = 35\n     \\]\n\nSo, the result of \\( 5 \\cdot 7 \\) is \\( 35 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{35}\n\\]\n\nIf you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask! <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation a